\fBpgrep\fR scans the running processes and prints the PIDs of those
that match \fIpattern\fR or the supplied filter criteria. By default,
the pattern is matched against the kernel's short \fIcomm\fR name for
each process (see \fI/proc/[pid]/comm\fR in \fBproc_pid_comm\fR(5));
with \fB\-f\fR, it is matched against the full command line as
recorded in \fI/proc/[pid]/cmdline\fR.
.PP
Filter flags select processes by parent PID (\fB\-P\fR), process group
(\fB\-g\fR), session (\fB\-s\fR), real or effective user (\fB\-U\fR
/ \fB\-u\fR), real group (\fB\-G\fR), controlling terminal
(\fB\-t\fR), or run state (\fB\-r\fR). Filters and a pattern can be
combined; a process must satisfy every constraint to match. \fB\-v\fR
inverts the match. \fB\-x\fR requires an exact match against the
whole \fIcomm\fR (or full command line under \fB\-f\fR) rather than
treating the pattern as a substring search.
.PP
With no flags, output is one PID per line, sorted ascending by PID.
\fB\-d\fR changes the delimiter, \fB\-l\fR appends the process name
to each line, \fB\-a\fR appends the full command line, and \fB\-c\fR
suppresses the listing in favour of a single count. No matches produce
\fB0\fR followed by a newline and exit status 1.
